<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haqnazar Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1538">1538</a>–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haqnazar Khan was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> from 1538 to 1580 and a son of Qasim Khan. Coming to power after a period of defeats and internal strife, he restored Kazakh power over the steppe, fought the Moghuls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> in the east and pushed west into former Nogai pastures as the Nogai Horde weakened. His long reign made the khanate again the dominant power between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haqnazar-khan.jpg" alt="Haqnazar Khan" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Padishah_(Emperor)_of_Dast-i_Qipchaq%22._Tabriz_or_Qavin,_circa_1550._British_Museum,_1948-10-9-056_(complete).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haqnazar, also known as Haqq-Nazar, Khaknazar Khan or Ak Nazar Khan, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasym-khan">Kasym Khan</a> and Khanik Sultan Khanim and the younger brother of Muhammed Khan. For much of his early life he went by his middle name, Haider, given in honour of his father's Shaybanid uncle Sheikh Haidar; he took the name Haqnazar, from the Arabic for truth and the Turko-Persian for vision, only on becoming ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>. During the reigns of Taiyr Khan and Buidash Khan he lived under one of the Nogai princes, and he assisted Taiyr Khan and Tugim Khan in the First Kazakh Civil War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the deaths of Taiyr Khan and of Tugim in 1538, Haqnazar took the title of khan and seized the territories of Ahmed Khan in the west and of Buydash Khan in the east. The khanate then faced rivals on every side: the Nogai Horde in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-sibir">Khanate of Sibir</a> in the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a> in the south. He brought the northern regions of Saryarka back into the khanate, retook Saray-Juk and the lands around it from the Nogai Horde, and in war with the Khivan Khanate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> won the Mangyshlak Peninsula.</p><p>His campaign to bring Jetisu into the khanate ended in defeat when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> joined the war on the side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>. In the north he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuchum">Kuchum Khan</a> of Sibir, reunited the Kazakh tribes and became the sole ruler of the khanate, the first since Taiyr Khan, receiving the Persian title Shah-i-Turan, King of Turan. In 1570 he defeated the Nogai Horde at the Battle of Emba and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, where Russian forces drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> back. His rule of more than 40 years brought the khanate back towards the power it had held under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasym-khan">Kasym Khan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haqnazar Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Haqnazar Khan</b> (r. c. 1538–1580) restored Kazakh power after a period of defeats, and Tauekel Khan seized Tashkent, Turkistan and other towns from Bukhara in 1598, gains held under his successor Esim Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kazakh Khanate. Died in Turkestan, in what is now <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.37,67.96&amp;year=1559" aria-label="Turkestan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Haqnazar Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5524 4184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5524 4184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.37,67.96&amp;year=1559">What was here in 1559? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
